2. Records of each source inside the database
---------------------------------------------

Every record carries the licence of its source in its `licence` column, and
the table `sources` of food.sqlite (also dist/manifest.json) gives the full
name of that licence, its address and the attribution to reproduce:

  off      Open Food Facts           ODbL 1.0 (database), DbCL 1.0 (contents)
  usda     USDA FoodData Central     CC0 1.0 (public domain)
  ciqual   Ciqual (Anses)            Licence Ouverte / Open Licence 2.0 (Etalab)
  swiss    Swiss Food Composition    opendata.swiss terms "Open use. Must
           Database (FSVO)           provide the source."
  cnf      Canadian Nutrient File    Open Government Licence - Canada
  cofid    CoFID (United Kingdom)    Open Government Licence v3.0
  kratheos Kratheos and its users    ODbL 1.0

The files dist/generic_foods.* hold no record of Open Food Facts: someone who
takes only the records of one of the other sources from them may reuse those
records under the licence of that source alone, with its attribution.

3. The source files stored in this repository
---------------------------------------------

The files under sources/ (and those of mirror/ attached to releases) are the
publishers' own files, stored exactly as downloaded. Each remains under the
licence of its publisher, listed above and in sources.toml together with the
sentence of the publisher that allows redistribution. Next to every file, a
.meta.json sidecar records where and when it was downloaded and its checksums.

4. kratheos.sqlite
------------------

The Kratheos layer (products, corrections, confirmation counts and
translations contributed through Kratheos) is part of the database above and
is made available under the same Open Database License. It holds no personal
data: no account, no handle, no identifier of a person.

5. No warranty
--------------

The data are provided as they are, without warranty of any kind. Nutrition
values come from publishers and from product labels entered by volunteers;
records that fail a plausibility check are flagged, not corrected. Do not rely
on them for medical decisions.
